The problem asks us to count the number of pairs of mochi $(A_i, A_j)$ such that $A_i$ can be placed on top of $A_j$.
The condition for placing mochi $A$ (size $a$) on top of mochi $B$ (size $b$) is $a \le \frac{b}{2}$.
The mochi are given in ascending order of size: $A_1 \le A_2 \le \dots \le A_N$.
We need to find the number of pairs $(i, j)$ such that $A_i$ is placed on top of $A_j$.
Wait, the problem says "You choose two mochi out of the N mochi, and place one on top of the other".
Let the chosen mochi be $A_i$ and $A_j$.
If we place $A_i$ on top of $A_j$, the condition is $A_i \le \frac{A_j}{2}$.
If we place $A_j$ on top of $A_i$, the condition is $A_j \le \frac{A_i}{2}$.
Since $A$ is sorted, $A_i \le A_j$ implies $A_i$ is smaller or equal to $A_j$.
If $A_i \le A_j$, then $A_j \le \frac{A_i}{2}$ is impossible unless $A_i = A_j = 0$, but sizes are $\ge 1$.
So, if $A_i \le A_j$, we can only place $A_i$ on top of $A_j$ if $A_i \le \frac{A_j}{2}$.
We cannot place $A_j$ on top of $A_i$ because $A_j \ge A_i > \frac{A_i}{2}$ (since $A_i \ge 1$).
So for any pair of indices $\{i, j\}$ with $i \neq j$, there is at most one valid orientation.
